   15   \cf1\f1 This is a common situation.  OS8CON depends on the underlying writing method of the OS/8 and P?S/8 handlers agree with each other. There are only a few cases where this is NOT the case.  For example, if you use a byte-mode-oriented handler in one system but not the other, OS8CON could not be used  between the two systems/devices.  OS/8 and P/S/8 both agree that DECtapes and LINCtaps are logically the first 128 words of each block [but OS/8 references the two blocks together logically as a record].  Thus, this works for the RX, the RK, all DECtapes and LINCtapes, etc.  Arguably, the comment is superfluous and should be in only the EXCEPTIONS, but it is not wron.  In the case of RX, this also means logical layout as well as both using 12-bit mode.  this means using block 0 is on track 1, sector 1.  There was also a P/S/8 effort to use track 0 to get more space, but this would not be compatible, etc.  The comments in the various bootstraps below bring up a bit of this because I not only designed it, I told DSD about it, etc.  Sometimes perfomance per se is the most important thing and OS/8 inter-compatibility drags down the result, but there was never wide-spread support for that variant, and then the RX50 pretty much killed that idea, allocating track 0 and 78 and 79 for DECmate slushware requirements.  But in theory, could still use byte mode and interleave variations, etc.\par
