\cf1\f1\par
This notion of calling it an RX8B is somewhat obsolescent.  It will be changed in the future to just RX8E and then the extensions will just fall into place as needed.  It's another aspect of the presence of the obsolescent DSD-210-related support that emphasizes that it is useful to write-protect when the system comes up.  OS/8 cannot do that by poor design leaving it to only an expert can tell WHY the system halts, so to bring up OS/8 you have to ALWAYS write-enable yet be at risk if the process fails!  [Again, yet another OS/8 bad design point!]  Technically, the SEL instruction is NOT part of the RX8E, but it is the extension to get to additional driver pairs on the VT78 and all DECmates.  What it does is model dependent, but the handler does the correct thing for the RX01/02 drives and not the RX50 drives where relevant.  On the RX8E it doesn't do anything, which is fortunate because on a DW8E-based older system IOT 0 is not supported.  But on the VT78 and the DECmate I it allows a second pair of drives if the AC[11] bit is set.  On the DECmate II it also sets the aAC[0] bit to force the RX01/02 and NOT the RX50.  This is why one physical RX01 media diskette can in theory be run on ANY model with 8K or more on the straight-8 through the DECmate II with the proper hardware configuration.  [Family of 8 only of course!]  OS/8 cannot do this because it needs 12K, unavailable on the LINC-8 and damn hard on the 8/L.  Note that the PDP-12, as in the basic 8/I, has 8K internally, but both machines take memory "wings" in expansion cabinets to get one or two more fields up to three wings to get an extra 24K to max out at 32K.]\line\cf0\f0\par
