\cf1\f1 The NL0006 cannot run on earlier models.  In theory, an RX8E could be in a DW8E-N on an 8K straight-8 or LINC-8 and thus is in theory an option to not have to modify the LINC-8 to be bootable, but these are very rare configruations in fact.  Yet, they used NL0006 as part of the standard bootstrap!  I rewrote it to be shorter, and one word longer to eliminate the 8/E dependency, but on balance the shortening wins out.  The purpose of the bootstrap is to manually toggle something in.  The standard bootstrap is "fancier" and as such, has to be one word longer to be functional prior to the 8/I.  The take-away on this is that by then, no one was caring about family of 8 any more.  Actually, in theory they mostly did, but there was so much frenzy over just getting the RX8E to work in a one-page handler, that was the only consideration at first.  Of course the handler didn't work and has the dubious distinction of being the only handler officially withdrawn because it was broken and could not be fixed without making it downright unreliable [but noticable] and an army of angry users would be out there.  The unfixable bug in that extremely low performance handler that required the 8/E instruction set was this.  If you get an error of any kind, then if it is a write, it can EASILY write out the WRONG DATA and worse report NO ERRORS!  This is downright dangerous, and some files I got at a DECUS symposium in fact had the corruption.  The self-test replaces the contents of the silo with track 1, sector 1. But the flow of the handler is such that if this happens, it retries now with the wrong contents!  Thus, if on the second or third attempt it "works" the data is now permanently wrongfully written.  Since we do now what is generally in the tested sector, I have shown files that are EXACTLT clearly the victim of this handler!  But if you don't retry at all, you will invariably get read errors in marginal situations that if there was the retry count of 3 it would properly read on retries.  the media is even DEFINED to have a soft error rate that is significent!  So, either you withdraw the handler or you set the retry count to not retry at all because reads and writes use the same retry code, etc.  That way, you don't get bad data, but you get the serious consequences of too many read errors.  Truly unacceptable either way.\line\line So, the code was not fixable, but it was too late, part of the smart-ass "short" bootstrap for such as the MI8E is not compatible with the straight-8 and LINC-8 and that is unacceptable.  But the whole point is they were too focused on getting something POOR BUT FUNCTIONAL to work in 8K; the 12K concept came later when they were forced because of the TD8E, THEN back-filled with the acceptable RX8E handler that needs 12K.  It is still SO PATHETIC there is no room for it to set the SEL instruction!   Thus, when the VT78 handlers came out for the VT78, they had to make THOSE HANDLERS exit by reseting with SEL to drive pair 0 lest the system handler not be able to fix it!  Yes, it is THAt tight in 12K  As I have pointed out, 12K OS/8 is feeble in many cases.  This is unfixable, so the non-system handler copout became necessary, etc.  And if you run some user program that fails to reset it, the system handler will fail!  And I can't fix it in OS/8.  Of course THIS handler has no such problems and supports the entire family of SEL-based configurations of 4-drive systems and also the incompatible DSD-210 way to get to four drives.  In short, their code is crap and mine is a tour-de-force on how to do everything correctly.\line\line At the time we did not yet have the 1K extension in mind, and it easily fits if the extension was to be .5K.  But clearly with this handler, the RK handler that needs a two-page buffer within it, etc. it became obvious it has to be rounded up to a full 1K extension, and the RX50 handler may be the one that hopefully just fits in all those pages because SO MUCH has to be done on the DECmates because of the sappy design of the ROM code, etc.  And as I explained, I can't even get to this until I implement the next generation of the non-system handler to support an "internal" mode function RAW WRITE sector as passed and do not check for stealth tactics, etc.  [Something has to write it out initially!  You can't implement the protections until you have something to protect!  Thus, when the system supports a non-system handler to create the O/S, it will have to force this mode where necessary.  Because we are going to double-precision arguments, I will just define a high-order all bits set "impossible" block call to mean to do the unchecked write.  And even perhaps specify the byte extensions in case that is necessary as well.  [Believe it or not I have a way to get around that problem!  I believe I have a 12-bit pattern of words that WILL PASS the ROM check!  It still means you have to prevent FUTURE CHANGES to the contents of the block in general, but if you force THESE VALUES every time, it should work correctly.  P?S/8 still needs the stealh regardless because some of these words want to be modified, so the stealth copy has to be used regardless.  The nuanced small point here is this:  If this magic pattern actually works, then I don't need to somehow support a byte-mode buffer for writing it to make the one-off even more one-off that makes the handler even longer.  [Note:  This is still talking about the NON-SYSTEM handler, which in general has to honor the stealth mode.  The handler when initializing has to read in the contents of logical block 0 where the affected sector data lives whenever the handler is asked to access logical block 0000,0000 [double-precision notation!] and then set a flag that says to stealtholy protect the important words.\line\line I just realized there is a fatal flaw in this reasoning!  It can be fixed by defining an additional restriction:\line\line All extended handlers reserve not only 8 pages in 8 blocks for the guts of the handler extension, but also two pages tus two more blocks as "helpers".\line\line Thus, now I have to create a special case:  If this is a machine with an RX50 on it, regardless of what the system device actually is, some small part of one of the helper blocks has to be reserved for the stealth function.  Consider a system based on the RD50 hard disk.  That handler will also be complicated, but it will NOT require a stealh component because there is no comparable problem.  The mechanism for bootup for the hard disk is to locate the hard disk and that the RX50 drive 0 is not ready.  This is all done by the ROM and includes searching for a named volume on the hard disk called FIRMWARE [not really quite an apt description, but it is what they did.  RD50 works with named volumes in ASCII text names, etc.  Silly frill, but it is what it is, etc.]  The actual programming is remarkably close to the RX except for geometry, remember NO DMA on a DECmate!]\line\line Then because the RD is being used instead of the RX50, they have to find and load the FIRMWARE volume or else you stil cannot boot to the hard disk.  Once you get past this [and there are extensions to Master Menu to hand this off, so it can be done for P?S/8 as well] THEN you get to actually boot using very familiar RX-style code, so it will have all the same problems.  But this avoids all of the stealth issues and why those commands in OS/278 V2 actually work fine IF the device is the hard disk.\line\line Thus, all I have to do is define a universal place where the helper block that holds the magic words "lives".  On the RX50 system itself, no problem, just use the defined blocks.  But what if you want to allow access to block 0 via a non-system handler.  How do you get to support a write update with the stealth protection?  Thus, it is necessary to define another reserved area on ALL systems that might apply to allow the non-system RX50: handler to have a place to store the data for the lifetime of the handler being in memory.\line\line OR\par
\par
every time you write out block 0, you MUST write out the stealh-protected data as well AND invalidate that you have it in memory.  This SLIGHTLY lowers the performance of the non-system handler, but it means it is a self-contained device hnadler.  [Just store it within the handler while it is in memory.]  Thus, every write sets the invalidation switch; you can no longer trust you have the stealth data in memory.  A new operation for a read loads in the stealth data as it is, and every write knows it has to write out a proper composite the same way the system handler does.  The difference is that the system handler can optimize to store a proper copy of what is on THIS system is in fact on this particlar RX50 diskette, etc.  It's a very subtle design point, but as long as I use something that works, it will be fine!\par
\par
[This is why I like writing this stuff down;  makes me think about nuanced issues!]\line\line In any case, none of the above applies to the RX01 handler!\line\line A future issue:  Do I have to have an RX02 variant of the system as the only way to actually boot a double-density diskette?  I think that this is actually quite necessary because things are so different compared to the world of RX01.  A NON-SYSTEM handler that can read or write either is a different matter, and that of course can be done.  The present handler considers the presence of the double-density media an error.  The system handler MUST do that as it does not.  But a future NON-system handler could embrace that and do the appropriate double-density stuff the way the OS/78 V4 non-system RX01/02 handler does.  [Remember, we are looking for a theoretical release for OS/8, but it is not showing up!]\line\line In any case, this means little will change in THIS source code file!  The non-system handler as written mimicks the logic here, and that would be changed in theory, etc.  A lot depends on whether or not I can get SIMH to support RX02 AND tell it there is a double-density diskette in it.  Not clear how this is done, but this has to be eventually checked.  long-term back-burner project after a lot is done first!\line\line Thus, NL0006 is defined in their poor bootstraps, and my variants avoid using it so they are fully compatible.  But these bootstraps are defined below and all the variations that were developed.  Several of them are quite useful beyond the theory.  I have in fact used the one that DSD embraced. [I wrote some of this for them for free.  They led me on the trail to get to Intersil back in the day, etc. and they knew Lenny Elekman who I was out of contact with at the time, but that has been since corrected more recently, but again, he has somewhat disappeared.  He has a long-term weird history, etc. including politically being all over the map as well, etc.]\par
\par
