  134                 /               IS EVEN A DEPENDENCY UPON A PORTION OF THE PRIMARY CODE WHICH IS\par
  135                 /               NEVER OVERLAYED!\f1  \cf1 This is a VERY SERIOUS MATTER.  Most bootstraps are designed to overlay everything that was brought in initially to get to the next boot-up level.  This is in part WHY there is the stealh issues in the RX50 handler!! Yes, all of the handlers just imitated this piss-poor notion that started at the very beginning and no one ever fixed any of this!\par
\par
When you do not overlay part of the code that gets the next level in, you now cannot redesign that code because it now has to be there and stagnates the design!  Thus, as the bootstrap unfolds, that code has to be there on the media as well!  You cannot use that part of the disk for any other purpose.  But in P?S/8, that has to be massively different from the stupid fixated bootup requirement.  Had it been designed better, it could bave been moved to a more suitable area, but they were so selfish and shortsighted and blinded and desparate to get something up that worked in 8K, they literally didn't pay attenotion.  It was something "close enough for government work" while they feverishly worked [in vane!] failing to get a proper handler for all the effort, then had to abandon it because of the fatal bugs and require 12K.  But it's now too late, everyone is stuck with this poor and rather selfish boot convention.\line\line So, when P?S/8 comes up, so much of the handler is just not in memory, that is instead the crappy non-overlayed code so that SOMETHING can come up at all.  Then what comes up is used to get even more stuff up until there is eough up to go back and fix it all in memory, etc.\line\line Fortunately, the serious part [the words that have to be modified] are outside of this area [and are actually in the locations that DO get overlaid or else this would also be a "stealth" problem!\line\line The only flaw in it is that what is generally wanted is that the system loader, the code at 07632-7637 thus cannot contain by default a call to save all of field 0 and then start at 7600 to do a warm no-write reboot as in all other systems.  So, that has to be restored by more support stuff in the 1K section.  [It's not a big deal, just another detail to be fixed up, but it is annoying.]\line\line Thus, when the /I option gets most of the system device handler back into memory, it has to call a routine to then also restore the system loader so that the [actually likely] attempt to call the system loader to write out all of field 0 is available.\line\line This is but another example of the "extras" that will have to go into the RX50 handler as well, making it effectively a little smaller for all the OTHER stuff it has to do!  In essence, all annoyances at the same time; not so many for the RX01 etc. and why it got done in just under half of what became available later.  We hadn't yet finalized the 1K max size notion when this was written, just do the best you can, etc.  But as time wore on, it became clear we had to reserve even more as the general trend was going downward!  This was primarily because the PDP-11 had easier standards to write their handlers for, thus they could deal with progressively poorer hardware design that even more depended on software, and the PDP- got the short end of design AFTER they did for the -11 what they could get away with to minimize what the hardware had to do, etc.  Eventually that backfired with the software-controlled stepping for seeking the RL01.  RT-11 had to create a "fork" task so that the RL handler had access to the mandatory clock-timer portion of the system so that it could wait for the requisite time you had to and then poll to make sure the heads were not seeking, and then check for a cylinder error in case it was wrong.  RT-11 runs with interrupts and cnanot just sit there for major fractions of a second incapable of doing anything else.  Of course since PDP-8 systems are generally non-interrupts, the handlers indeed do nothing but wait, but at least they can be interrupted by an applcation that sets interruptss, and the penalty is potentially lost dosk performance, etc.\line\line But the point is, that even the -11 people were shafted by allowing lazier hardware people to save pennies instead of doing better designs.\line\line Then the -8 never supported the even-newer disks and they were all run by code written by Richard Lary inside! This made the mediocre -11 systems work better despite the inherently poor disk controllers.  Thus, this just vindicated poor hardware design still further.  RT-11 could talk to those disks, but had to deliberately only make one call at a time, which is not what it was optimized for.  Thus, RT-11 relatively speaking crawled on these disks muddying the waters so that RT-11 didn't seem as superior to the clumsy things like RSTS and RSX-11, etc.  Not that I ever cared about -11s, but the point is this is part of how companies go down hill and then go under, etc.\line\par
\line Instead of better O/S design, as long as it was multi-tasking, the disk innards made the decision to allow the disk to do as much as it could even with poor seek performance.  Richie's code does "elevator sorts" so that the current set of tasks at the moment are done in the best order for the current disk itself, a dynamic decision based on a static determnation of the disk geometry.  It makes those crappy systems seem better for all toe wrong reasons, and it allowed DEC to sell crappier performing disks and unfairly compete with actually BETTER disk designs!\line\line Of course, eventually we go microprocessors on hard disks, etc. which more leveled the playing field and the Gizmo in theory takes a little advantage of that. But hindsight is always 20/20.  My points are always based on the then-current decisions made which were so badly performed and predictably what would happen did, and that part is NOT 20/20 hindsight!  Many saw it coming, etc.\line\par
Management killed DEC, not technology.\par
