\cf1\f1 Even with the relative luxury of a boot-time-only logical block zero area, not a handler, they didn't have enough room to start at 07605 so that OS/8 would not write on the disk, so that you get yet another possible error needlessly.  Thus, it was not safe to write-protect the disk.  DEC didn't have any write-protect and only that user mod even could enable it.  But the DSD-210 has the nice front-panel write protect switches on the drive, so it mattered to them.  Thus, I was explaining the better design where you can safely write-protect until it came up, but as DEC wrote their crappy original code, you had to be an expert to know if it was safe to press CONTINUE and a better design, if it had more room could avoud it, but because of the selfish short-sighted design, they ran out of space even in a boot-time only area; it was literally not quite big enough to do it correctly, so it had to do it half-assedly thus, the better hardware was moot because it took too much user education to be of any practical use.  But I could redesign the boot process to be safer, even for OS/8; this comment is referencing that, but perhaps not in enough detail.  In any case, this handler is layout and data compatible with OS/8 so OS8CON works as well here as it does on say the TC01/08 etc. and it could be made compatible with a smarter safer bootstrap that could be used for either system with write-protect universally.  It would involve writing on track 0 which DEC foolishly declared "hallowed ground" you couldn't change due to a massive misunderstanding of what and how IBM's usage of the media actually worked!\line\line In truth, it was entirely irrelevant!  But they were idiots:\line\line On the IBM key-to-disk unit, there was not quite a "directory" as we understand it, but they did allow certain sectors to be written using the "write deleted data" function [which is actually useless but supported!].  Thus, it had to scan the entire disk to find out what was recyclable if they otherwise ran out of room.\line\line Track 0 was a validation and bitmap of this activity, and thus was intrinsically needed to allow precise COPYING of the device because you had to be prepared to write the "deleted data" headers where needed to make a true clone copy of their disks.\line\line Moreover, to format the media on the IBM machine, there was a "user" mode and a "diagnostic/technician" mode to erase the disk.  The former checked if track 0 contents were consistent with what was there, as a check/requirement of formatting, which ws really just a rewrite all sectors as NOT deleted data and with a standard empty byte pattern [I believe every byte was writte with E5 hex but I could be mistaken].  That would then be an "empty" disk as far as those machines were concerned.\line\line But the "technician" mode involved opening up a side panel [!] and throwing a REAL format switch.  It did NOT care what was on the disk before, it is assumed this is the first-time format, etc.\line\line But because of stupid companies like DEC which couldn't have their hardware format, they easily got to resell IBM brand preformatted diskettes!  That's why they were so expensive until the third-parties such as MAxell came along to do it far cheaper, etc.\line\line So, they actually decided track 0 was off-limits for 100% irrelevant reasons and that is totally stupid!\line\line Since the DSD-210 could format [I wrote the program] this was all irrelevant, thus my point about a DSD exclusive to make both operating systems come up from safer code that is written to track 0 that really no one cared about [other than the ignorant assuming DEC knew anything about it!].  Thus, it was a safer rebooter after a boot to the code on track 0 was first booted to with a different piece of raw boot code, etc.\par
