\cf1\f1 This was one of those right hand doesn't know what the left hand is doing.  DEC never completely eliminated the write protect bit and there was a user mod to actually implement it by the hole blocked by foil method.  DSD also supported that media-based protect, but also on the front panel of the drives, write-protect switches, thus on the DSD drive you could see this as a feature both ways.  What I didn't write here is that even in the UNMODIFIED RX01, this bit also comes up if the drive is not ready!  It is not unreasonable that a drive not ready is also set to write-protected.  You needed media to block the photocell light beam, etc.  Thus, ANY RX01 has the rudiments of this write-protect bit despite  bad documentation apparently to the contrary, and certainly, no one in the RX02 project ever got the message, etc.  So bad was this that they ASSUMED it was a "new bit" to implement, so they made it "This is an RX02" which is pathetic given what it REALLY means!  Thus, a CORRECT definition is:  IF the drive is an RX01, THEN the bit means either the drive is write-protected OR the drive is not ready.  IF the drive is an RX02, then it means the drive is an RX02 [yes, THAT worthless; the intent is clearly to tell the difference easily, instead it creates a nightmare of incompatibility!.\line\line To make matters even worse, inside of the RX02 drive, and I first found this on a table-top model where you can open up the top cover to easily reach the switch, and this was intentional because of so much early software could not deal with RX02-isms at that early stage of RX02 production, they implemented a compatibility switch inside.  With that set, you cannot use double-density media at all, and thus, the RX01 bootstrap such as in the MI8E will work fine to boot a single-density media.  But it gets even worse.  It still has the "since this is an RX02 the I am an RX02 bit is set" bit is set and write-protect cannot be implemented at all.  Thus, only IGNORANT software works on the drive set that way!  But if you use programs that care and do things because of this, the programs FAIL because the RX02 protocol will not work but the drive claims it IS an RX02.  But it only RUNS RX01 software!  This is just weapons-grade stupid!\line\line Programs such as RTFLOP were in strong usage then.  I was using it and getting into trouble on the DSD-210 because of the write-protect; I had to make sure the foil was set to the write-enable mode AND also make sure the write-protect switch was set to write-enable, else the program would assume to use RX02 double-density mode!  Yes, the program is incompetently written per se, but it's because this is one of the "victims" of the conventional wisdom game.  Similar to the one where the deaf leading the dumb leading the blind invented a "fact table" of how device 03/04 works on PDP-8's versus on DECmates.  The problem is it is 100% WRONG!!!!\par
\line The point is the application writer DEPENDS on being told proper relevant information.  Then the fairy tale turns into the nightmare of the reality, etc.  And this is in programs of size, NOT handlers where there is a space restriction.\line\line Thus, I can fix them if I have the source code, etc.\line\line The heart of the fix is to do the special function read status in double-density mode.  That is not supported on the RX01 group and it succeeds if the drive is ready AND there is an actual double-density disk in the drive.  It FAILS with an error if on an actual RX02 with a single-density diskette inserted.  Thus, doing nothing = RX01, error= not ready OR single-density media present and no error = RX02 and double-density media inserted.  This is all accomplished by ignoring the bit.  If the function does nothing, the drive is an RX01, and in that case, the write=protect bit applies unless the drive is not ready, which causes a different error.  Thus, ignore the stupid bit and perform a few tests that are say about 10 words apiece,  Then you know the true status and can deal with it.  But they spent all of this design effort and grief inventing an anomolous bit that in reality just gets in the way.  Then the kludge in the RX02 as implemented just makes it even harder, etc.  Yet, OS/8 works largely because the handlers are too stupid not to!  Of course RX02 software all just plain fails in that situation.  But the P?S/8 handlers are ALWAYS properly aware of the problems.\par
\par
When you use RX01 protocol in byte mode, you are in 8-bit mode on an RX01, but it is 16-bit mode on an RX02 with double-density media, else it is byte mode if the media is single-density.  The programming is different and these feeble programs typically just hang when confused.  The 12-bit mode is the same for both of them, but the sectors are twice aslong in double-density mode so you have to transfor twice as many words before the done flag raises [and you do have to make that happen; you are obligated to fully empty or fully fill the silo accordingly.\par
\par
