\cf1\f1 DSD never actually made one for the PDP-8. Unfortunately, they combined it with a Q-bus interface all in one module, so only for the PDP-11.  But it did carry out compatible operations, so that the RT-11 drivers actually work fine.  But since they never did one for the -8, all we have is the OS/8 handlers for the in-house prototype.  I implemented the "two sides present" bit in this handler, thus it can work with double-length disks, but still single-density only.  Of course, somewhere I had at least a CANDIDATE handler for OS/8 if not the "official" one because this was handed to me by Stan Rabinowitz.  We know it ought to exist, but the relevant part is in the source code for OS/78 V4.  It is just broken with regard to Control-C handler as is everything from OS/78 V4, but otherwise is likely the identical code.  I can of course derive what the changed code is, to put it back to what it should have been, but of course that would not be authentic and there are those issues about the RESORC bits, etc.  I can't help James Zune on that score, and as I said, there are unknown additional compatibility issues with ANYTHING from OS/78 V4, especially for Michael Thompson trying to use the code on a PDP-12 and not on an 8/E or newer!\line\line That's why the only way I can really effectively help any of them AND also not spend too much time doing what ought to NOT have to be done at all [the file should just exist] yet we cannot find binary nor source code, so I cannot really help.  If I had the binary, i would attempt to guesswork a restoration, but I  have to have the binary to disassemble from at least!  Then, I can use the too-new source code and back-track to a pretty close source file.  But so far, no one seems to have it, and rememmber I couldn't find it looking at Dave Gesswein's collection where I was looking for them and the RL drivers for Jack Rubin.  He has no need or desire for the KT8A extension and would rather just run OS/8 V3D 3Q everywhere.  I think I can make the RL handlers be more conforming, but so far, no sign of the right piece of code.  You can run the too-new stuff on the hardware, but it won't do Control-C correctly and of course it can be used on SIMH, etc.\line\line This is not my battle; get me the tools I require and I will spend a little time on it, without even the binary, leave me alone.  But maybe something will turn up somewhere.  Even a copy of BUILD with the correct handlers in it will suffice, but so far, all are the too-new ones from OS/78 V4. I suspect ignorant tossing away of the proper files in favor of the "new" ones because everybody knows [!] that new =  better, right!  [Fools for years believed that in OS/8.  Turns out they copied OLDER files without a date set in OS/8. Guess what happens when you forget to set the date?  The copied file becomes TODAY's date of the transfer when you copy the undated file to another system and DO set the date.  Thus, these so-called "newer" files were of course actually usually MUCH OLDER but fools believed the date OS/8 indicated, not really knowing their sources of the file were inherently unreliable!\par
\line The P?S/8 SHELL will NOT suffer from this!  OS/278 V2 actually FINALLY got this the same as P?S/8 always has been.  The date is ALWAYS set unless the system is virgin.  If you perform an explicit set, it is permanent until another explcit set command is performed.  Thus, worst case you have a reasonably close date, which is a lot better than no date at all.  But OS/8 never permanently sets the date, thus all of those problems.  I can force the issue by releasing the SHELL sstem with the date set, even if it 01-Jan-1900. Of course internally it will have the concept of undated, but the point is that I can make the scenario that is in OS/8 moot and of course, a file date should ALWAYS stay with the file unless and until you MODIFY it, in which case the CHANGED file gets today's date as set in the current system, etc.  But OS/8 never did that, thus the high possibility you can NEVER TRUST a file date as meaning ANYTHING unless you can independently verify it!  That is the harsh reality!  [Of course, yet another nail in OS/8's coffin yet fools still don't listen to me by and large.]\line\line I can't help it if too many people have low expectations, but this is nothing new at all.  As engineers, we strive always to do better, not accept poor-to-mediocre if there is a reasonable way to do better.\par
