\par
\cf1\f1 I have to revisit this.  I need a more bullet-proof way to establish the current bootstrapped logical unit.  On such as the TC01/08, it is easy:\line\line The process of booting up is a trick:  It looks like there is a call to the handler to do the impossible, namely read into memory block 0 over itself.  [It starts out that way, but as the code DMA read happens, it modifies itself to do thw correct thing.\line\line In any case, this means that at 07602 you have to have a value with the following bits set:\line\line Bit[0] must be clear so it is a read not a write!\line Bit[1-5] can be set to anything at all.  One page is enough to overlay the code in memory with new contents from the read in progress to make it boot itself accordingly, etc.\line Bits[6-8] have to be 000 because that is the field for the read.\line Bits[9-11] have to be set to the bootstrap logical unit needed elsewhere later as required.  On the extended bootstrap code in Version 8Y and up with an extended bootstrap I wrote, P?S/8 can actually boot DECtape to ANY unit!  The last part of that bootstrap is to set location 7602 in memory to 0200 plus the unit in the low-order bits.\line\line But in the RX system that has to be part of the damn bootup words!  Thus, it cannot be used for the later purposes.  This code is zeroing it out so that if you want to, after the starter system is created, you can restart at 07600 and actually boot the new system for the first time, etc.  As a restriction, the creation of the system is always to unit 0 [and maynbe that is ALWAYS the only case!].  that is thus necessary, etc.\line\line What I am now thinking for Version 9A, is that IF this can be true because the handler has to fit in all of 07600-onward ONLY so it can run in 4K, this is likely the right thing to do on such a system.  But if you need more memory, then perhaps have some extended memory convention to find out.  It can easily be figured out, but this is a bit more complex than in Version 8Z, but these are all once-only initialization code considerations so a saner approach that is a few more words is acceptable.\line\line A problem case will be the TD8E with MR8E-C ROM system after I fix it to work properly!  It may not be able to suppor the 7602 convention the way it attempts to now once I fix the bug.  Hopefully I can make it conform as well.  Just have to store 0000 in 7602.  The boot itself will be in 7600:\line\line CIF 70\line JMP I something like 7603 where 7603 contains 7470, meaning let the ROM reboot the system always.\line\line Just another minor consideration fixing that handler that never really worked before!\par
\par
But that has to be done because it is a 4K-capable system!  [No extended memory to force an alternate reference to!].\par
