  851                 /       P?S/8 SYSTEM I/O ROUTINE LOADER.\par
  852   \par
  853                 /       CORE IMAGE OF A BINARY FORMAT FILE WHICH WILL BE WRITTEN OUT TO THE /I BLOCK.\par
  854   \par
  855                 /       THE FOLLOWING PAGE OF CODE IS AN EXAMPLE OF A P?S/8 BINARY FORMAT FILE.\par
  856                 /       IT WILL BE WRITTEN OUT AT SYSGEN TIME AS A CORE IMAGE OF A "FILE" TO BE\par
  857                 /       USED LATER AS THE LAST LOADED "FILE" WHEN INVOKING THE '/I' OPTION OF THE\par
  858                 /       SLURP LOADER. AT RUN TIME, IT WILL BE LOADED OVER AND NEXT TO THE SLURP\par
  859                 /       LOADER (BY THE SLURP LOADER!), CAUSING THE SYSTEM I/O ROUTINES TO BE RELOADED;\par
  860                 /       THE FINAL EFFECT IS AS IF THE VIRTUAL LOADER (/V) HAD BEEN USED, BUT WITHOUT\par
  861                 /       THE OVERHEAD AND WRITING (THE SLURP LOADER AND /I CODING CAN BE USED WITH\par
  862                 /       WRITE-LOCKED DEVICES.)\par
  863   \par
  864                 /       THE ONLY RESTRICTION ON USE OF THE /I OPTION IS THAT IT IS PASSED AS A FILE\par
  865                 /       ARGUMENT TO THE LOADER, AND THUS LIMITS THE LOADER TO ONLY SIXTEEN FILES\par
  866                 /       MAXIMUM INSTEAD OF THE NORMAL SEVENTEEN. BY USING THE /I OPTION, THE USER\par
  867                 /       CAN MAINTAIN DEVICE INDEPENDENCE, YET TAKE ADVANTAGE OF THE BENEFITS OF\par
  868                 /       THE SLURP LOADER OVER THE VIRTUAL (/V) LOADER.\par
  869   \par
  870                 /       P?S/8 BINARY FORMAT AND CORE IMAGE FORMAT ARE RELATIVELY SIMILAR, BUT THE\par
  871                 /       DIFFERENCES WILL BE DESCRIBED.\par
  872   \par
  873                 /       P?S/8 BINARY FORMAT DESCRIPTION.\par
  874   \par
  875                 /       THE P?S/8 BINARY FILE FORMAT CONSISTS OF A SEQUENTIAL LIST OF BLOCKS, EACH\par
  876                 /       BLOCK CONTAINING 128 LOGICAL WORDS. THE WORDS BREAKDOWN FURTHER INTO EIGHTEEN\par
  877                 /       GROUPS OF SEVEN WORDS PLUS TWO WASTE WORDS. IF ANY PHYSICAL ADDITIONAL WORDS\par
  878                 /       EXIST, IT IS THE LOADER'S RESPONSIBILITY TO PROPERLY IGNORE THEM, JUST AS\par
  879                 /       IN SEVERAL SYSTEM HANDLERS.\par
  880   \par
  881                 /       EACH GROUP OF SEVEN WORDS CONSISTS OF A CONTROL WORD AND SIX DATA WORDS.\par
  882                 /       THE CONTROL WORD CONSISTS OF SIX BIT PAIRS EACH OF WHICH CONTROLS THE\par
  883                 /       CORRESPONDING FOLLOWING DATA WORDS. THE ORDERING IS LEFT TO RIGHT, I.E.\par
  884                 /       THE HIGH-ORDER TWO BITS OF THE CONTROL WORD CONTROL THE DATA WORD IMMEDIATELY\par
  885                 /       FOLLOWING THE CONTROL WORD; THE LOW-ORDER TWO BITS OF THE CONTROL WORD CONTROL\par
  886                 /       THE SIXTH DATA WORD FOLLOWING THE CONTROL WORD. SINCE EACH GROUP (AND\par
  887                 /       THEREFORE BLOCK) IS AUTONOMOUS EXCEPT FOR IMPLIED ORIGIN AND FIELD, ANY\par
  888                 /       SINGLE BLOCK CAN BE LOADED INDEPENDENTLY.\par
  889   \par
  890                 /       THIS /I BLOCK CONFORMS TO THIS CONVENTION, AND WILL BE TREATED AS A ONE\par
  891                 /       BLOCK LONG "FILE" COMPLETE WITH <EOF>.\par
  892   \par
  893                 /       ALL ACTUAL DATA WORDS ARE IDENTICAL TO THEIR CORE IMAGE COUNTERPARTS, HOWEVER,\par
  894                 /       NOT ALL WORDS IN A GROUP ARE ACTUAL DATA WORDS.\par
\par
\page\par
/     P?S/8 RX01, ETC. SYSTEM HANDLER         P?S PAL V08S    SUN  16-JUL-17  PAGE 24\par
\par
  896                 /       BIT CONVENTION FOR CONTROL WORDS.\par
  897   \par
  898                 /       EACH OF THE CONTROL WORD'S BIT PAIRS CONTROLS THE CORRESPONDING DATA WORD\par
  899                 /       IN THE FOLLOWING MANNER:\par
  900   \par
  901                 /       00      ACTUAL DATA WORD; THE CORRESPONDING WORD IS AN ACTUAL DATA WORD\par
  902                 /               TO BE LOADED ACCORDING TO THE CURRENT ORIGIN ADDRESS; LOAD IT AND\par
  903                 /               ADVANCE THE ORIGIN ADDRESS BY ONE.\par
  904                 /       01      END OF FILE; IGNORE CORRESPONDING WORD (AND REST OF FILE!).\par
  905                 /       10      ORIGIN SETTING; SET CURRENT ORIGIN ADDRESS TO THE VALUE OF THE\par
  906                 /               CORRESPONDING DATA WORD.\par
  907                 /       11      FIELD SETTING; THE CORRESPONDING WORD IS A CDF INSTRUCTION TO THE\par
  908                 /               NEW FIELD; EXECUTE IT WHEN LOADING DATA WORDS INDIRECTLY THROUGH\par
  909                 /               THE CURRENT ORIGIN ADDRESS. THIS ALLOWS LOADING TO OCCUR IN FIELDS\par
  910                 /               0-7.\par
  911   \par
  912                 /       TYPICAL P?S/8 BINARY FORMAT FILES ARE CREATED BY EITHER AN ASSEMBLER PROGRAM\par
  913                 /       OR THE BINARY UTILITIES (BIN, BSAVE, ETC.). SEVERAL SYSTEM PROGRAMS CHECK\par
  914                 /       BINARY FILE FORMAT VALIDITY BY LOOKING FOR INITIAL DATA OF AN ORIGIN TO\par
  915                 /       0200, THUS MAKING THE CONTROL WORD OF THE FORM 10 XX XX XX XX XX, AND THE\par
  916                 /       FIRST DATA WORD 0200.\par
  917   \par
  918                 /       THE /I "FILE" WAS NOT CREATED DIRECTLY BY ANY ASSEMBLER, AND THIS LACKS THE\par
  919                 /       (UNNECESSARY) "*200" CONVENTION OF A DEFAULT ORIGIN. ONLY THE SLURP LOADER\par
  920                 /       WILL EVER LOOK AT THE /I "FILE", AND THE LOADER DOESN'T VALIDATE THE FILE\par
  921                 /       FORMAT.\par
  922   \par
  923                 /       A TYPICAL BINARY FILE IS SLIGHTLY GREATER THAN 7/6 OF THE EQUIVALENT CORE\par
  924                 /       IMAGE FILE SIZE.\par
  925   \par
  926                 /       SINCE NO CHECKSUM SCHEME IS USED IN THE FILE FORMAT, THE USER CAN PATCH\par
  927                 /       FREELY ANY BINARY FILE. THIS IS USEFUL WHEN MODIFYING OLD PAPER-TAPE BINARY\par
  928                 /       FILES WHICH LACK FIELD SETTINGS, ETC. WHEN ATTEMPTING TO LOAD THEM INTO\par
  929                 /       EXTENDED MEMORY FIELDS THEY WERE NOT INTENDED FOR, ETC.\par
  930   \par
  931                 /       THE /I CODE COULD BE IMPLEMENTED BY A USER PROGRAM, BUT WOULD THEN NOT BE\par
  932                 /       DEVICE INDEPENDENT.\par
  933   \par
  934                 /       SPECIAL NOTE ON SELF-STARTING.\par
  935   \par
  936                 /       SINCE THE USER CAN DIRECTLY OVERLAY THE LOADER (INDEED THE /I OPTION USES\par
  937                 /       THIS VERY METHOD!), IT IS POSSIBLE TO CREATE A "SELF-STARTING" PROGRAM BY\par
  938                 /       SELECTIVE OVERLAY OF THE STARTUP LOCATIONS:\par
  939   \par
  940                 /       BCIF (07746)    STARTING FIELD AS A CIF CDF INSTRUCTION.\par
  941                 /       SPARM (07756)   STARTING ADDRESS IN THE STARTING FIELD.\par
  942   \par
  943                 /       USER LOADING OF THESE LOCATIONS WILL ALWAYS OVERRIDE ANY AND ALL OTHER METHODS\par
  944                 /       (DEFAULT VALUES, DEFAULT VALUES WITH /I IN EFFECT, "GET" VALUES, EXPLICIT\par
  945                 /       COMMAND OPTION SWITCHES, EQUALS PARAMETERS, PRIOR USER LOADING OF THESE\par
  946                 /       SAME LOCATIONS IN AN EARLIER PASSED BINARY FILE, ETC.).\par
  947   \par
  948                 /       N. B. THIS SELF-STARTING ABILITY IS TOTALLY IGNORED WHEN USING THE VIRTUAL\par
  949                 /       (/V) LOADER; SEE "BIN" FOR A DISCUSSION OF THE DISPOSITION OF USER LOADING\par
  950                 /       INTO 07600-07777, ETC. WHEN THE VIRTUAL LOADER IS IN EFFECT.\par
\par
