\cf1\f1 The code above is a bit nasty to do from extended memory because it would take a lot of field-change gyrations.  Thus, it is split at the best possible point to avoid the grief.  It's hard to do the equivalent from another field because the data field is the caller's field that is still needed at this point.  But if you want to do the equivalent of TAD I SYSTEM you have to do that indirectly, which means that the data field has to be saved, set to field 0, do the equivalent, then set it back to the caller.  Thus, this disposes of all of the caller's inline arguments.  Then the data field can be anything you need it to be, etc.  Notice the RDF below.  After that is accomplished, the data field is set to 0 so that the SEXIT can be stored into.  Then it's just keeping the data field at field 0 unless actually transferring the user's data, etc.  That's the norm in any program transfer code, but remember, the caller has its own field to get the arguments from while the field for the transfer itself is contained withim the function word's bits [6-8].  This allows any combination, but to implement it, this wold be a problem if the code were NOT in field 0, etc.  The rest is not a problem per se, etc.\cf0\f0\par
