\cf1\f1 This part came about wheh I added on the RX03 support by mimicking what is in that OS/8 handler.  I don't know it it is from the actual exact version we are looking for, it could have been a pre-release version Stan Rabinowitz handed to me, etc.  In any case, clearly there WAS a release of what we are looking for for the benefit of James Zune, etc. but only have the too-new one for OS/78 V4.  There should be a proper set for OS/8 V3D/3Q if for no other reason that Stan was still there, but would leave soon thereafter to work for the Typesetting group [not Typeset-8, this was a VAX group and it did much different work and by then, paper-tape was passe in that world].  OS/78 V4 came out for the DECmate I as the first system that was incompatible, and Stan was already out of there, not that he could have fixed much of it, but the point is the new "young turks" with their idotic notions basically start there and the era of Stan ends just before that.  They had no "respect" for V3D and indeed just recycled the OS/78 name stupidly.  That is NOt what it meant up until then, they just co-opted it.\line\line OS/78 was just the stupid brainchild of that moron Ron Janzten we spoke about.  OS/78 was coincident wuth the VT78 and the fact it was 1978, marketroid stupidity overload.  And remember, the claim to fame of OS/78 was a) A couple of frill features that were already in OS/8, just that no one knew about it, and there was a command SET SYS OS8 and another SET STS OS78 to emable many of them, which was merely the inserted use of esc sequences that work on a VT5x terminal hear and there, and in no rational way or order.  Later they did something like that in DECmate OS/78 to declare it VT100 because the DECmate I was in a VT100 style case, etc.  But these are silly cosmetics, but not carried out very well or even consistently.\line\line An idea I had was to have somewhere a place to store what unix people call termcap.  It's not worth wasting much space on, but I don't have any current ideas what or how to do it.  I have to room in the basic 4K system kernel for a universal set of bits to rule everything; they couldn't do it in OS/8 either, so they just make certain things work differently and then only programs that do not completely destroy the keyboard monitor can know what to do.  In P?S/8, the SET command makes the keyboard monitor embrace a smattering of this, etc.  Maybe after I get in the low-level monitor I could have a place for some of that as well, but there is no chance for a central place to do such as that.\line\line There is an alternate sticky point in the design:  If you have such as an LA36, how do you tell programs to NOT create fake tear-off lines and instead output a real form-feed?.  I solve that in the console overlay by declaring the LA36 as also the LPT:.  Thus, if you asn for a listing from PAL, it starts with a <FF> and as long as you do not interfere with the output [other than control-S/Control-Q) you get a clean printed output.\line\line But there is no place in the system to for example declare that the console is always to be treated as a lineprinter for all output being formatted, or all output might want to use clear the screen escape sequences, etc.  In OS/8 it was just somme silly stunts and not well used.  So, in all probability, P?S/8 will not do any of this unless the SHELL is in place, since it can write its own "rules" into the communications and info 256 word area, etc.\line\line In any case, the rest of "OS78" was Janzten's folly, as I explained where he added the "less is more" notion of forcing th have only the CCL variant of the commands work and NOt the noraml ones based on R or RU\{n\} and also prevent all the related commands.  It was monumentally stupid, and we destreyed him at DECUS by typing in "CCL" which is the CCL command to turn itself off so you MUST use the forms he has now locked out.  Thus, his system at that point is 100% useless!\line\line Some people did buy it becasue it also included some minor programs that are for the VT78 sort-off, not really only there.  But they do make sense [he didn't write any of them, just as the asssitant manager and dabbling programmer the stupid restriction thing.  These other marginal, albeit useful programs are this:\line\line OS/8 was modified so that EVERY comforming program that loaded onto page zero [I REFUSED to conform in Kermit-12] reserved location 00000 and 00001 to be the essence of a hardwired interupt handler.  It uses CIF 30 to go to field followed by a JMP somewhere on page zero.\line\line The idea was that if interrupts were on, then every aspect of OS/8 could be interrupted and a master interrupt handler was running in field 3.\line\line Tere are some problems with this:\line\line 1) It means you have to have exactly 16J,  no more, no less because the VT78 is non-expandable.\line\par
2) No DMA devices because they could overlay page zero during an interrupt and thus desroy the interrupt return to what was interrupted and thus corrupting permanently the in-memory system [at least, hopefully a rebot fixed it!].\line\line The programs invented a SPOOL "handler" to output to instead of a printer where a printer was appropriate.  Then you had to run the SPOOLR program to enable the interrupts and load a piece of code into field 3 and of course proclaim the system only has 12K [which ruins such as PAL8 assemblies that might be longer, etc.]\line\line The notion was that if you use the contrived feature, you could see that in the background, the slow printer was slowly craning out the line-printer-intended printing overlapped with further program execution initiated by new commands to the keyboard monitor.  Impressive to newbies, but you cannot hide the fact that the VT78 was THAT slow because of the bad handling of the RX01, but this was desperation time!\line\line P?S/8 funs fine on the VT78 and yes, it is a bit slower, but not THIRTEEN TIMES as slow as OS/8 is.  WPS also works fine because it does internally its own everything, no standards to have to deal with, etc.  Part of it is in byte mode and I think it uses 3:1 interleave or whatever that is NOT going to be causing rotational latency, etc.\line\line I'm not saying WPS is "intelligent" in any sense, just that it is not trying to use the OS/8 standard way to access the disk that works acceptable if the RX disk is on an 8/E, etc.  Thus, they MUST have used a different disk mapping scheme such as 3:1 interleave, but I really do not know exactly what.  But it was DESIGNED for the VT78 [and called WT78 which means WPS keybap colors].  Thus, this was the beginning of them soft-pedaling OS/8 on the latest model, and of course then the end happened with the next model because that was the first DECmate, etc.  It was the end of an era ended with pathetic attempts to continue, all of which were flawed, some flawed more than others, and some were "self-inflicted" because those losers had the nerve to change code that was not broken, thus they broke it needlessly, and of course never fixed a lot of it.  I have discussed all that CANNOT be fixed because OS/8 has no space, but now I am referring to changes that should have NOT been made because the change only broke OTHER thngs and fixed nothing, save the one small good idea of writing out the date as we discussed, etc.  [And I give credit because that was hard, given the other problem where it becomes unbootable.  The only answer is they stored a permanent copy somewhre else and the copied it into memory corresponding to what WOULD be written out, but wasn't.  Arguably, this is a tiny essence of my steatl technique dependind on exactly what they did, but not really and not expandable;  I need several words steatlthed, not hust that one, etc.]\line\line\line I expect what they did was to write it out on one of the blocks of the OS/8 keynoard monitor which is acceptable to patch, then as it comes up, it copies the saved word into memory of the kernel.  But the corresponding word on the actual diskette is never updated, etc.  You can do that to the OS/8 keyboard monitor, and arguably the feature could be retroitted to OS/8 itself, etc.  Just that you don't have to indirectly carry it out in OS/8, a direct write to the corresponding word on the disk would be fine for other than the RX50, thus fine for OS/8 [that doesn't run on DECmates!].\line\line It has to be something like that due to the hardware debaclle of maintaining boot validity, and it has to be in their kernel so the programs that look at it can.  This is probably in the main source file of OS/278 V2 which is available even on sunsite/ibiblio, etc.\line\line In any case, OS78 bever meant anything particularly "good" and it is sorta poetic justice that OS78 V4 just adds additioanl grief and quite a lot of it as incompatible HARDWARE forces things to not work [and bad programmers make things even less functional; however it took until OS/278 to REALLY ruin many things so badly.  There is no SA?NE reason that typing in <LF> in OS/278 ODT hangs the machine!  And it is a very important command in ODT!\line\line I use it all the time in OS/8 and P?S/8.  It means close the latest memory location if you had changed it, and then open the next location automatically without speficying where to go intrinsically go to the next, etc.  With that not working, ODT is SEVERELY broken!\par
\line This is a sign of idiots mangling code, which we know they did in OS/278 Version 1 which was never completed and neve worked, and then newer people came back to try to rescue it back to closer to wher eit started [again, if it ain't broke don't try to fix it, so since they dd break it they tried to fix it, but didn't quite get it back to where it had been before the former iditos broke it needlessly, etc.]\line\line I can only imagine whatelese was broken such that this command hangs, yet most of the other commands work!  There is too little code to that part of ODT for it to be so specifically screwed!!\par
\par
There is no reason why the ODT stuff should not be identical to OS/8 with the exception of the control-C routine which of course only partially works as expected.  This is probably the way the code was only partially modified as in OS78 V4.  But instead OS/278 V2 has "exquititely bad" bugs that it takes "talent" to fuck up so badly!\line\line This section just gets the other-side head bit set if appropriate when you access the backside of a two-sided diskette in the RX03.  It was a quick and dirty add-on.  ARguably, some of the field 0 parts of the handler that still actually are in field 0 could be slightly reorganized for clarity and less sections like this, but in no oher way does makng any changes amount to anything, etc.\par
