\cf1\f1 The NOP after the HLT is just a place-holder.  The page was rewritten several times until the right amount of what is needed in field 0 actually fit.  Above, in comments you see stuff about go somewhere else and do the equivalent of this.  That refers to sections of code in the handler extension that in fact to what it says.  But I had to make sure there was at least one location available somewhere below  7750 to hold a pointer to somewhere within the Logical Console OVerlay so it could change the HLT to a JMP I .+1 and then the intercepting address.  When the Overlay is enabled there are lists of addresses and replacement contents [and even the proper contents to put back if you officially turn the Overlay off, so it cleans up after itself.  Each device has to have a custom set of these of course, generally one that intercepts earlier before anything untoward happens just so it knows what to do if needed, and another at the HLT to then carry it out which can include embellishments on the output, really nice to watch if the device is a VT8E or other terminal emulator kind of system where the normal console screen can be temporarily replaced with an error handling screen and then go back to the normal console output screen.  The PDP-12 version will also look pretty nice when I write it, etc.  If you are just writing to the normal 03/04 device, you get interspersed error messages.  No nicer way to do it.  In any case, this is the vital place where that is initiated on any form of error.\line\line Of course I could have just reserved another address to serve the same pointer purpose, but I was adjusting what fits whaere, and if any changes are made, just have to go throuugh it all over again.  This way it is reserved inline the way it is often done elsewhere.  Also, this is a sticky point.  If it were not exactly there, the next word has to perform a  CIF into extended memory to accomplish the actual error recovery code.  Thus, overall this looks better and takes no additional words despite the fact that it looks like there is an "extra" word.  In theory, if I declare the code final then I could change it and have said pointer location elsewhere, but regardless there is no free space!  The S70 has to be in  7747 explicitly, thus it has to be at a lower addrees, but in this little area, there ARE no other words free!  Thus, overall this is the best to make the most sense for all of the contingencies, but I admit it is counterintuitive at first glance.  Of course, another way would be to make the code fragment in the comments even longer so what is in field 0 has free words, and then it could easily be separated because then this section could start at say one address earlier, etc.  Some of this is borne of necessity and some of it is just programming style and in any case trying to use appropriate "style cosmetics" to be appropriate for the situation.  This is not something that comes up all that often, but when you write handlers that cannot fit in one page, it is the expected norm.  Even OS/8 two page handlers have some baby versions of this problem [but they don't also have the console overlay problem to deal with!]\cf0\f0\par
