\cf1\f1 As I explained, this is the minimum size memory needed to asssemble this file, not how much memory was available.  All available memory is always used up to whatever could accommodate as many as 4096 symbols.  In general, that would be four full memory fields beyond field 0, but if you use /C then a part of field 0 is also used, and I did set that switch.  This means the input buffers are diminished and thus the assembly takes longer, the output buffer for binary output is also diminished for the same cause.  If the logical console is not enabled, then the first 3/4 of the highest field is also potentially allowed.  This would only apply if the system device requires an extension in extended memory [such as this RX01 etc. handler!].  Of course it was assembled with the TC01/TC08 handler so the highest field was totally available because this doesn't apply.  Of course if the Logical Console Overlayy were enabled, then NONE of the highest field is available.  In any case, this is a part of the dynamic allocation performed by PAL during the once-only initialization code, etc.\line\line So, instead of the confusing statement of what the assembler COULD have used regardless of the size the program mght require which adds little useful information, this is the amount of memory it *COULD* have been successfully assembled into worst case.  Notice it has to be specified to the nearest [rounded up] ONE K because of these considerations.  For example, this assembly could have been done on an 8K machine of course, but in this case it could ALSO be on an 8K machine where the last 1K is not available because of the device handler extension stored in the top 1K.  If this had said 8K, then it would take a 12K machine given that scenario.  This is also affected by the number of modular assembleer options set, such as LINC mode dual assembly, which was NOT set in this case, nor would it have been purposeful to have done so.  Literals are used, so that module is necessary.  There is also a one-page "neatness" module to make the listing file better [headers, etc.].  If you don't mind a "rough" appearing listing, it could be done in one less page.  Thus, all of these factors go into the determination to the nearest 1K as stated.  If this was only barely into 7K territory, even turning on the dual mode assembly *might* have fit in 7K as it occupies 1/2 of the 1K space add-on in theory, etc.  [1K is 8 pages of memory; the dualmode assembly is the largest module at 4 pages.  Literals are 3 pages because it has to store the extent tables and the page zero literals entirely during the assembly, which could be as large as 0020-0177 AND 1/2 page minus 1 for the latest current page buffer.  And then the extent table for 31 potential current pages, all of this at the same time, as well as implement the parsing changes to support literals at all.  All of that barely fits in three pages!  It also includes a routine to "dump" the literals into both the binary output and the listing output at the end of the assembly OR at any FIELD setting during the assembly.  [VERY busy three pages in that module].  [Note:  All modules are dynmically page-relocated, since where exactly they execute is a function of just how many of the options are loaded in any particular assembly.  Fortunately, the relocation is done by once-only initialization code.  The technique is adhered to "religiously" so that the code that REMAINS in memory is as small as possible, even if the code to make it function is longer [as that is "for free"].\line\line A minor point of the assembler:  Part of why it is fast is not obvious.  I think PAL8 has to self-reference where it is loaded from when another pass starts.  P?S/8 PAL has a second-round of once-only code for the purpose of starting up pass 2 of the assembler.  That once-only code is itself initialized at the beginning of the assembly to make sure it is also as short as possible.  This was a success that was hard to achieve but it was!  The code is stored in the memory that later is used as the most minimal length of the binary output buffer.  Thus, when between passes, this code sets up what happens after that.  Thus, this secondary once-only initialization code was "for free" as well, but it is not "infinite" as it has to fit into the two-page minimum write buffer when binary output is being created soon thereafter, etc.  A lot of that code is itself subject to relocation and is patched to be what is needed.  In fact, most of it is a patch table of address followed by replacement value OR multiples of that with a count, etc.\line\line In any case, this is totaly successful so that there is no going back to read in some part of PAL to accomplish further initialization.  This is an incredibly subtle and nuanced part of the design, but it is just another part of just how powerful PAL is as a "savvy" program design.  I doubt if any other larger-scale program ever written by anyone goes this far to spare memory.  No other P?S/8 program has "two-stage" initialization.\line\line However, after the end of the second pass, if cross-reference /X is enabled, it does have to swap in more code to sort the references.  This is the part not yet done.  And actually fortunate because it has to be redesigned.\line\line The original design for reference dump is too crude: It demands free reign over every block of arbitrarily logical unit 7, and for very large assemblies, this is just not enough!  So, it is being changes to a variable number of extended-length output files, from potentially each of the eight such extended-length files, one on each logical unit including the system bootup device.\line\line But as a practical matter, probably no more than three could be needed, even for humongous assemblies.  Remember, the space needed is not a function of memory in the same sense as the symbol table allocation, rather this is a matter of just how many STATEMENTS are in the assembly.  [Comments are ignored!].  In general these go hand in hand, but if you add more references to the same number of symbols, the memory to assemble is not affected.  But a longer assembly has more references to perhaps the same number of symbols relatively speaking, etc.\line\line Thus, this will be a Version 9A consideration.  The former failed extended-length file DIRECTORY concept will be removed and replaced with the better idea of a SINGLE extended-length file per logical unit, and efficiently from several vantage points the info is located within the end of the system programs directory [since in general it is only accessed when that structure is in memory during the R and RU\{n\} commands only.  The SET program will be modifed to add management functions for the extended-length files themselves, such as naming them by the user.  [Note:  If the extended-length file's name is anomolous to a TFS file, it will NEVER be possible to be accessed as an input file for any program execution!  Thus, the user has to have the ability to set this name uniquely and outside of their personal naming convention of TFS names.  I have them patched to such as LONG2, LONG3, LONG4, LOMG5, LONG6, etc. on the PROVISIONAL usage support in Version 8Z, which is essentially just making sure that the highest actual TFS file has no entries higher up in the directory.  That way, all the blocks from where that TFS file is and higher are available [up to the highest block in the extended-length file directory such as it is.  That information is valid, but otherwise the structure is literally totally wasted as it was never otherwise implemented!  That vital value will be moved to the end of the system programs directory and the blocks will be repurposed, most likely to the low-level keyboard monitor, a new concept I invented because in theory you can lose control of the system beween a) The Logical Console Overlay is enabled on a portable device such as DECtape, but brought to a machine with either less memory or lacking the hardware altogether, thus the system cannot come up, OR the SHELL is enabled and located on physical blocks that do not exist because they are on some other logical unit that didn't come along for the ride so to speak.  The low-level monitor will run in just 4K and require no more than the standard 03/04 console.  It will then be able to turn off either feature.  Then, the system can come up normally, allowing re-customization if relevant.  This rarely happened in the past, but it DID HAPPEN.  By providing this feature, I can actually save a SMALL amount of memory in the keyboard monitor once-only code [the part that is what is in control just before command input is allowed, and if I get some of that space back, I can use it to do something else, such as perhaps fixing that one obscure bug we never noticed until quite recently.  [Using :u on both the command and a file argument; they both work, but when you use both at the same time, it doesn't work!.  I tried a patch and it did help, but it also screwed up the ED\{it\{ command badly, so I removed the patch.  This is something VERY obscure and will take a lot of time and effort to figure out what is the obscurity causing the bug.  Most likely inconsistent use of a few page zero locations or somesuch.  The fix may be trivial, but it's not know at this time, etc.]\line\line The SET command will setup a set of access bits used only by PAL for /X reference dumping purposes.  It won't be literally starting at block 0000, but a minimaal skeletal system can easily start at something like block 0160.  Then the rest of the blocks up to in theory 7777 are available on EACH of several extended-length files available for the purpose.  Thus, two of them is far larger than the old notion of every block on unit 7 and nothing else.\line\line Paradoxically, you have to be somewhat judicious in use of the extended-length files for this purpose.  Too many and they are wasted, because long assemblies by nature must have the source material located in at least one of these same extended-length files.  Thus, there becomes the practical compromise of just how long a source program size can be accommodated while the more there is needed there, the less is available for the reference dump!  But since it will be dynamically assignable, it should be capable of working for any project of any size that makes sense.   Remember, I have experience with a few very large assemblies, including several that OS/8 just runs out of gas on!  You have to know what you're doing, but the point is you will be able to do things OS/8 is just totally outclassed. [Note:  Way down the road, the SHELL will be able to do this easier from the user's standpoint, but needs lots of resources.\line\line From the standpoint of the code within PAL now, this is a small change.  All it needs is a short routine to setup the logical units starting and ending blocks which is at most 16 words by definition.  Then as one unit's capability fills, go onto the next extended-length file's starting and potential maximum block.  [Note:  There is no need to have a forma end-of file indicated anywhere.  When the reference dump is written, there will be an end-of-data code so it will known when to stop, etc.  The unwritten routine to sort the dumped elements will need a copy of the same exact table.  I will attempt to pass the info from one overlay to another, but worst case, it can always read in the last block of the system programs directory on the boogup device and rederiving the identical information, etc.  Cross-referencing is messy and trying to be "too efficient" for all purposes will not work.  The average assembly will only need the first starting and ending block.  Thus, to save memory, perhaps just passing TWO WORDS so it can get started is good enough so that most of the time it does save time at the expense of inevitably the savings is not obtained on the truly humongous cases, which at first glance seems to be a proper design.  two words saved instead of 16 words might just fit.  Notice that currently, the starting block is 0000 and the end block is obtained from the obsolete extended-length programs directory.  Thus, this is not all that much different, but it is specifically different.  In any case, this is for the next release, etc.\par
