\cf1\f1 This is a literally correct map of what the binary output looks like.  Remember, the initial system creation has arbitrary memory locations in 12K-16K where the images of blocks are stored during the initial system creation process.  This has no bearing on when or where they are loaded LATER, and in some cases, that's actually multiple considerations.  For exampl, the BIN program first loads some of the code relative to where BIN as a system program loads when first executed, but within that progam image are sections potentially LATER moved to yet other places!  Thus, you have to be intimately familiar with all of the various "levels" of the loading; this just addrssses exactly where in the 16K at the creation event this stuff is loaded.\line\line This is part of why I want to make a simpler system creation mechanism that transitions from the way it MUST work now into a better way that can run in less memory, nearly in all cases actually 4K even if the system handler needs at least 8K.  [This is because a non-system handler will be part of the process.  The non-system handlers generally fit in a few pages, but could be defined as large as a field.  But as a practical matter, the ones that are analogous to the system device handlers that must be by definition nine pages or less, these non-system handlers will tend to be sligtly shorter or even moreso smaller, thus it is a safe bet that an appropriate non-system handler can be loaded into say 8 pages or less in a 4K program that otherwise fits in 31 pages.  That will simplify these assemblies.  One section is devoted to an absolescent task:  As written out by the requirements of booting, the system handler cannot function unless it is not only relocated to appropriate memory, but also a fixup routine is executed to that it can in fact act as the device to write all the whole starter system onto at all.  If a non-system handler is required, it just gets loaded and works by definition.  Thus, that part of this assembly will no longer exist.  In theory, I can accomplish this in parallel with developing to Version 9A, i.e., it could still apply to Version 8Z because it does not change WHAT is accomplished, rather it changes HOW it's done, etc.\line\line The above is the essence of why I need DTORG to work, and since I still require OS/8 to do it, I have to get OS/8 PAL8 to "work" in a humorous sense.  In actuality, this will be the BINCON program to create proper OS/8 binary that does what I want.  It starts with reading the defective output of PAL8 that has DTORG in it that makes the file impossible to load with OS/8 ABLSDR by design for those Typset-8 idiots' benefit.  But BINCON will be DTORG-aware and if so:\line\line 1) Turn off checksumming.  I am not sure if an equivalent checksum can even be calculated if even a single DTORG is used; it is THAT incompatible!  That has to be researched.\line\line 2) Look at the intention of the DTORG as they did it, and then place into the P?S/8 binary output the proper DTORG as I have defined it.  [And how in the future, P?S/8 PAL will in fact do it.  That will be needed when I get rid of OS/8 entirely obviously!]  [Note:  Only the starter system is affected currently, but I think I can create future extensions that affect adding in system programs as well.]\line\line 3) At this point, a P?S/8 corrected binary output file stream exists from the PAL8 assembly.  The BINCON program will be bi-directional, so that can be converted into a single .BN file that ABSLDR can process correctly.  Thus, this will allow me to optionally use DTORG statements as I want to, then get the output .BN file converted to be fixed so that it can be loaded, and then convert that back to OS/8 .BN file so ABSLDR can then actually load the fixed-up version.\line\line [Isn't it pathetic that OS/8 implements a misfeature that prevents it from loading its own files? And then I have to write a P?S/8 utility that fixes it up so it can anyway!  But there are also other useful reasons to write BINCON, etc.]\par
